DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The lliibbnnvv library permits easy management of name/value pairs and can
     send and receive them over sockets.  For more information, see nv(9).

     The ddnnvvlliisstt__ggeett functions return the value associated with _n_a_m_e.  If an
     element named _n_a_m_e does not exist, the function returns the value pro‐
     vided in _d_e_f_v_a_l.  Returned strings, nvlists, descriptors, binaries, or
     arrays must not be modified by the user since they still belong to the
     nvlist.  If the nvlist is in an error state, attempts to use any of these
     functions will cause the program to abort.

     The ddnnvvlliisstt__ttaakkee functions return the value associated with _n_a_m_e and re‐
     moves the associated element from _n_v_l.  If an element named _n_a_m_e does not
     exist, the value provided in ddeeffvvaall is returned.  When the value is a
     string, binary, or array value, the caller is responsible for freeing re‐
     turned memory with ffrreeee(_3).  When the value is an nvlist, the caller is
     responsible for destroying the returned nvlist with nnvvlliisstt__ddeessttrrooyy().
     When the value is a descriptor, the caller is responsible for closing the
     returned descriptor with cclloossee(_2).

